+### Issue #5 — YouTube anonymous extraction rejected by every configured client, root cause still unconfirmed (open, ongoing)
+- **Symptom:** during manual real-Telegram testing of the YouTube quality-selection flow (Step 7), `get_available_qualities()` raised `YouTubeExtractionError`. yt-dlp logged an identical `"Sign in to confirm you're not a bot"` rejection for all four configured anonymous clients (default, `android`, `ios`, `tv`) in `downloader/youtube.py`'s fallback chain. **As of this writing, this has now happened on every single real attempt: 5 different real videos, tested from 2 different countries (Moeid's own connection, and a friend's in Turkey) — zero successful downloads so far.**
+- **What's been ruled out:**
+  - **Not a single-video quirk.** A throwaway manual diagnostic script (`manual_test_youtube_fallback.py`) confirmed the identical total rejection on a second, independent real video, ruling out "this one video is flagged" as the explanation.
+  - **Not a stale yt-dlp pin.** `yt-dlp==2026.8.19` (the pinned version) was confirmed to already be the latest release on PyPI at the time of checking — upgrading alone would not have helped.
+  - **Not a missing JS runtime.** yt-dlp `2025.11.12+` requires an external JavaScript runtime (Deno recommended) to solve YouTube's JS challenge, and its absence produces a very similar-looking failure mode. Deno was installed and verified active (`yt-dlp -v` showed `JS runtimes: deno-2.9.7` and the "no JS runtime" warning gone) — **the exact same bot-check rejection still occurred, unchanged.** This ruled out the missing-JS-runtime theory as the cause of this specific failure, though installing Deno was still worth doing regardless (see §7).
+- **What's suspected but NOT confirmed either way:**
+  - **VPN/IP reputation.** Moeid's traffic (and his friend's, separately, in a different country) both route through proxy/VPN setups for censorship-circumvention reasons (see §7). The assisting model's working theory is that YouTube's abuse-detection may be scoring the exit IP(s) poorly, independent of client identity, video, or JS-runtime presence — this would explain the 100% failure rate across every variable tested so far. **Moeid has explicitly pushed back on this theory** ("I don't think it's VPN- or IP-related. It's more likely related to the method we're using") and considers it more likely something about the extraction *method* itself. **Neither theory has been tested directly** — the diagnostic that would actually distinguish them (running with the proxy/VPN fully disabled) has not been run, and may not be practical given Moeid's proxy is a functional necessity, not an optional toggle, for his network.
+- **Current status: unresolved, open, tracked as a standalone parallel work item (Track B — see §2 and `AI_COLLABORATION.md`).** The extraction fallback loop itself is confirmed working correctly every time (tries each configured client once, in order, no wasted retries, wraps the failure cleanly into `YouTubeExtractionError`, produces a translated user-facing message with no raw yt-dlp text leaked to Telegram) — **this is not a bug in `downloader/youtube.py`'s logic**, it's that YouTube is rejecting every anonymous request this environment has sent it so far, for a reason not yet pinned down.
